<CaseLayout
  slug="raised-on-the-blur"
  section="playground"
  eyebrow="Research paper · Writing"
  title="Raised on the Blur"
  summary="AI-generated media, the attention economy, and Gen Z’s challenge: how constant exposure to AI content is reshaping the way my generation processes media, and whether the proposed fixes can keep up."
  snapshot={[
    ['Type', 'Research paper + presentation'],
    ['Lens', 'Adam McKay’s Don’t Look Up (2021)'],
    ['Length', '16 pages'],
    ['Written', 'Spring 2026'],
  ]}
>
  <section id="opening" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="The question">The question</h2>
    <p class="quote">“Somewhere on TikTok, Instagram, or Facebook right now, a video is circulating that never happened.”</p>
    <p>
      Gen Z are digital natives: the first generation to grow up during the internet’s reign rather than its rise.
      This paper examines how constant exposure to AI-generated media on social platforms is reshaping the way we process
      information, and whether the tools being proposed to fix that are actually capable of doing so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="numbers" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="By the numbers">By the numbers</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>~55%</b><span>of the time, people correctly identify AI-generated content. Barely better than a coin toss.</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>3,000%</b><span>growth in deepfakes between 2019 and 2023.</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>$40B</b><span>in projected U.S. fraud losses from generative AI by 2027, up from $12.3B in 2023.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="argument" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="The argument">The argument</h2>
    <p>
      The paper traces generative AI from 1970s pattern theory to GANs and large language models, then follows the money:
      platforms built on the attention economy, with algorithms that learn what keeps each user watching and feed it
      back in an endless loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Don’t Look Up</em> serves as the lens: a preview of what constant consumption of false media does to a
      society, and my prediction of where we land if the issue isn’t mitigated.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no single fix, and pretending otherwise would misrepresent the problem. One concrete step already exists
      inside frameworks platforms use today: extend the restrictions that limit minors’ access to certain content so
      they also cover verified generative AI content.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hci" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Why HCI">Why it matters to me</h2>
    <p>
      This is where my HCI minor meets my marketing major. <strong>Human-Computer Interaction research can build
      mitigation strategies around how people actually use generative AI content, not how platforms assume they do.</strong>
      As the line between real and generated narrows, the question of who draws it becomes more urgent.
    </p>
